Y4 Debian board integration
==========================

This ARM64 package contains the MCU daemon, signed MCU maintenance helper,
bounded boot initializer, Realtek NIC factory controller, native kernel status
collector, standard NetworkManager profiles and the MCU display firmware.

A configured production Ed25519 trust/signing key and a PG module compiled for
the exact installed CIX kernel are required to make an installable package.
The compile-only developer build deliberately does not invent a key or create
an unsigned installable fallback.

Installation is passive: there are no preinst/postinst/prerm/postrm scripts,
Debian triggers, automatic daemon starts or programming operations. The package
contains enablement links for the three board services; they take effect at
the next ordinary boot. That boot may perform the explicitly configured bounded
factory initialization. Existing running services are not stopped at unpack.

Named legacy MCU owners are masked in the data payload. Other services,
unrelated NetworkManager profiles, /etc/resolv.conf and resolver ownership are
not rewritten. Regular package configuration is recorded as dpkg conffiles.
Existing live firmware-update state and credentials under /var/lib/y4-board
are never part of the package payload and survive package upgrades.

For full firmware image creation, board/install.py operates only on an explicit
disposable Debian12 root. It refuses / and source-tree roots, validates the exact
kernel configuration, then uses host dpkg --root with deferred triggers.
The package has no maintainer actions, and an existing y4-board installation
with maintainer scripts is refused before unpack. Host depmod writes the image
module indexes without loading a target module. The image package database
therefore records the installation normally; this is not merely an untracked
overlay of binaries.

The image's exact SHA-pinned historical vendor ifupdown file is archived before
retiring only the old board physical-interface entries. Changed administrator
files are retained and reported. The normal Debian loopback stanza is supplied
if missing from the pinned vendor input. No network service or target binary
runs while staging.

The network profile uses the existing CIX802.3ad single-port fallback when no
LACP peer replies; actual same-partner negotiation can enable the multi-member
group. The adapter uses NetworkManager APIs and never owns a second DHCP client.
Read networking.md for default-route/source-routing and DNS ownership details.

The bundled MCU package is signed and verified as part of packaging. Firmware
API updates remain separate from Debian package installation and are verified
again by the target MCU maintenance helper. Only the raw public trust key is
included; private keys and local signer configuration are excluded.

This delivery has compile evidence only. No package installation, target
program, unit/integration test, QEMU run, device operation or physical board
verification was performed for this task. Complete runtime and board acceptance
in the integrating chat before claiming those scenarios passed.
